  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure: Infrastructure for Resilient Island States Phase 1</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Founded in 2019 the Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure is a global partnership working to strengthen climate and disaster resilience in infrastructure through knowledge exchange, technical support, financing, research opportunities, innovative solutions and access to international best practices. CDRI is rapidly growing, currently with 64 members comprising 52 countries and 12 organisations including MDBs and UN agencies. At COP26 the Prime Ministers of India, Australia, Fiji, Jamaica, Mauritius and UK launched CDRI&#x2019;s now flagship Infrastructure for Resilient Island States program. IRIS aims to strengthen infrastructure resilience in small island developing states, which are particularly vulnerable to devastating climate impacts, by deploying experts, sharing best practices and providing direct funding and technical assistance. At the launch PM Morrison announced Australia&#x2019;s $10 million commitment to IRIS.</narrative>
  </description>
